ce-insensitive EM would have masked the artifact issue, so the leading-space rule matters for exactly these lines.\n\n6) DEGENERATE CASES (researcher point 3), include-as-zero, never excluded: no-newline generation \u2192 its full capped text IS the first line (scored normally, and counted in a 'no_newline' diagnostic); empty or whitespace-only post-cut generation \u2192 first line \"\" \u2192 EM=0, ES per rule (vi), counted in an 'empty_gen' diagnostic; EOS-immediately handled as empty. Diagnostics table emitted per model\u00d7condition: truncation_rate, no_newline_rate, empty_gen_rate, mean_gen_tokens.\n\n7) PAIRED ANALYSIS (researcher point 4): greedy determinism means no seed variance, so inference comes from paired structure: (a) per-example win/loss/tie counts on ES (ties include both-EM-1 and ES-equal); (b) repository-stratified per-repo EM/ES means for both models to expose whether the gap is uniform or subset-driven; (c) bootstrap CI (1000+ resamples, fixed RNG seed 1234) on the paired EM and ES differences with the RESAMPLING UNIT = REPOSITORY (cluster bootstrap), declared rationale: completion examples from one repository share style, identifiers and retrieved files, so example-level resampling understates variance. Validated end-to-end in this sandbox on synthetic data: W/L/T=346/114/1094, diff=0.149, repo-clustered 95% CI [0.125,0.174] vs a nominally much wider i.i.d. interval \u2014 confirming the clustered unit is the conservative choice. McNemar's exact test on EM discordant pairs reported as a secondary.\n\n8) VERIFICATION CHECKLIST AT EXECUTION TIME (researcher point 6), blocking steps before any generation: (1) download official github.com/Leolty/repobench; open its generation eval script and metric file; adopt its exact prompt template string and metric file as reference-of-truth; if unreachable, freeze the fallback template/ES in \u00a72/\u00a75 into a hash-locked local file and document the divergence. (2) Open the downloaded Python cross-file 